Regulatory Compliance & Threshold Breaches:

Which cities, stations, or sites spend the highest number of days/hours in Poor, Very Poor, or Severe AQI buckets, and how frequently do individual pollutants breach safe thresholds?
Can we set up threshold alerts or drill-through views so operations teams can spot multi-day deterioration trends before they turn into regulatory notices or audit findings?
Where are monitoring gaps, missing values, or sensor dropouts skewing daily/hourly averages
How do we ensure compliance metrics aren't misread when fewer than three pollutants or no particulate readings are present?

Root-Cause & Pollutant Attribution:

Which pollutant is the primary driver of high AQI by city and season (e.g., winter inversion/stubble burning vs. summer/monsoon patterns)?
When AQI spikes, is it predominantly driven by fine/coarse particulates or localized gaseous emissions—and how consistent is that pattern across monitoring stations within the same city?
In hourly data, do peaks align with rush hours, shift changes, industrial activity, or meteorological shifts?

Multi-Site Prioritization & Resource Allocation:

If we have limited budget or intervention bandwidth across locations, how do cities/stations rank on both average burden and peak severity?
How do city-wide trends compare against station-level readings—are there specific micro-hotspots dragging down a city's performance, or is pollution broad-based?
If an action plan, dust-control measure, or operational change is rolled out, how cleanly can the dashboard show before-and-after shifts while accounting for seasonality?

Executive Reporting, ESG & Stakeholder Readiness:

How can we structure the report so an executive sees high-level KPIs and trends at a glance, while an environmental engineer or compliance lead can filter down to station, date, and pollutant sub-index detail?
Are AQI buckets, sub-index calculations, and missing-data rules documented and locked in the semantic layer so different teams don't report conflicting numbers?
How easily can leadership compare Year-over-Year (YoY), Month-over-Month (MoM), or baseline-vs.-current performance without getting tripped up by uneven date coverage across sources
(such as 2015–present city data vs. 2017–present station data)?
